Ear Syringing / Microsuction for Cerumen
General Practice Other
Sensation of a blocked/full ear, Conductive hearing loss, Direct otoscopic visualization of impacted cerumen, Immediate relief following removal

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe ear pain and sudden complete conductive hearing loss secondary to impacted cerumen following water exposure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden, complete unilateral hearing loss and severe, escalating otalgia that occurred immediately after swimming, causing immense distress and disorientation. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to differentiate acute otitis media from massively swollen, impacted cerumen, and to perform immediate therapeutic removal to relieve the acute pressure on the tympanic membrane. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in severe, unremitting pain and profound sensory deprivation overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I went swimming, water got in my ear, and now I'm completely deaf on that side and the pressure is agonizing."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a subjective sensation of a blocked or completely full ear that evolved rapidly over the past 4 hours. Following a swim, the patient experienced the sudden onset of profound conductive hearing loss on the right side, accompanied by severe, localized physical discomfort and a sensation of immense pressure in the external auditory meatus. The patient notes a new, loud tinnitus characterized as a low-pitched roaring. The patient admits to previous, chronic use of cotton swabs to clean the ears, which likely compacted the wax deeply. There is an absolute absence of purulent otorrhoea, fever, or prior symptoms indicating acute otitis externa. The patient has a known history of intact tympanic membranes with no recent middle ear surgery.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently highly distressed and disoriented by the sudden unilateral deafness.
Social/Safety Concerns: The acute sensory deficit is causing significant anxiety and loss of balance.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Distressed, holding the right side of the head, repeatedly asking the clinician to speak louder.
Physical Exam: ENT: Examination of the left ear reveals a normal, clear external auditory canal and an intact tympanic membrane. Examination of the right ear confirms the direct visualization of complete or near-complete occlusion of the tympanic membrane by a massive, dark, swollen plug of impacted cerumen using an otoscope. The wax appears hydroscopic, having visibly expanded after water exposure. There is no erythema or extreme tenderness of the pinna or tragus upon manipulation. Expected investigation results include the direct otoscopic visualization of an intact, healthy, and completely visible tympanic membrane immediately following the clearance procedure.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; the patient has been attempting to use a hairdryer to "dry out" the ear, which was advised against.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Ear Syringing / Microsuction for Cerumen (Acute Hydroscopic Impaction) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Externa ("Swimmer's Ear") - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe pain upon gentle traction of the pinna, lack of a swollen/erythematous canal wall, and no purulent discharge.

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms began instantaneously following water entering the ear, rather than a gradual viral prodrome, and the patient is afebrile.

Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct otoscopic visualization clearly identifying the obstructing mass as dark, typical cerumen rather than an exogenous object.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with acute, severe otalgia and sudden total conductive hearing loss triggered by water exposure expanding a pre-existing cerumen impaction. Telephone triage correctly recognized that the severe pressure and pain required immediate physical relief. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to otoscopically confirm the diagnosis, rule out an acute middle or outer ear infection requiring antibiotics, and perform immediate, gentle therapeutic irrigation. Removing the swollen wax plug instantly relieved the painful pressure on the tympanic membrane and restored hearing. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to excruciating, unremitting pain and sensory loss overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered cerumenolytic drops (docusate sodium) to briefly soften the impaction. Performed gentle, warm water irrigation of the right external auditory canal using a specialized ear syringe. Achieved immediate, successful removal of a large, solid cerumen plug. Re-examination confirmed an intact, pearly grey tympanic membrane and an immediate restoration of hearing and relief of fullness.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future, painful wax impactions, strictly cease the use of all cotton swabs (Q-tips) in your ears forever. Instead, simply let warm shower water run gently over your outer ear, then pat the outside dry with a towel. By replacing the damaging habit of inserting objects with a safe, passive cleaning routine, you actively protect your ear canals from physically ramming wax deep against the eardrum.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review PRN (as needed) if hearing loss or fullness returns in the future.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or attend the Emergency Department if you develop sudden, severe dizziness (vertigo) where the room feels like it is violently spinning, or if you develop sudden, severe facial weakness on the right side.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Your hearing is fully restored and the pain has resolved. The ear canal may feel slightly sensitive or "echoey" for the next hour, which is completely normal.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek routine medical review tomorrow if you develop a new, worsening ache in the ear, or if yellow or green fluid begins draining from the canal.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: You had a large build-up of wax that absorbed the pool water like a sponge, swelling up and completely blocking the canal. Flushing it out with warm water safely removed the blockage and released the painful pressure.
Actionable Steps: Never put anything smaller than your elbow into your ear. Keep the ear completely dry tonight; do not insert any more drops or use a hairdryer.

